WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.699
Start with the work that matters. Sarah checks workload, overdue reviews, and distribution holds before opening the queue.
THROUGHPUT: Put ownership and the next action in one place.

2
00:00:06.699 --> 00:00:20.684
Find the campaign. Keep its context. Search brings an overdue affiliate campaign into focus. Sarah opens the exact submitted creative.
INTAKE: Product, partner, reviewer, and due date travel together.

3
00:00:20.684 --> 00:00:41.104
The promise needs to change. The pre-check flags guaranteed approval and the credit-inquiry claim. Sarah writes specific revision feedback.
AFFILIATE EDGE CASE: Review both the ad and its destination wording.

4
00:00:41.104 --> 00:00:42.704
The next move belongs to Alex. Switch to the marketing owner. The campaign is waiting for a revision, not another email thread.
OWNERSHIP: Reviewers request changes; the owner edits creative.

5
00:00:42.704 --> 00:01:03.419
Revise the ad and the destination. Alex qualifies eligibility and explains inquiry stages in both texts, then submits version 2.
VERSION CONTROL: Changed creative starts a fresh review.

6
00:01:03.419 --> 00:01:07.779
Old copy stays. Old sign-offs do not. Both versions remain visible. Alex cannot approve the work; the decision returns to Sarah.
SEPARATION OF DUTIES: Authors cannot approve their own campaign.

7
00:01:07.779 --> 00:01:17.354
No flags is not clearance. Sarah opens Evidence and adds the product terms that substantiate the revised claims.
HUMAN DECISION: Automated checks are advisory.

8
00:01:17.354 --> 00:01:25.050
Evidence has a source and an expiry. Record a terms excerpt, source URL, and validity date. Evidence belongs to this creative version.
SCOPE: The source reference is manually captured, not independently fetched.

9
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:39.853
A reviewer must attest to the checks. Sarah verifies disclosures, substantiation, and distribution scope against the current evidence.
ACCOUNTABILITY: Evidence changes and reviewer handoffs reset attestations.

10
00:01:39.853 --> 00:01:45.629
Approval cannot outlive the evidence. December 31 is blocked because the terms expire December 15. Sarah corrects the date.
DATE BOUNDARY: The approval window is constrained by supporting terms.

11
00:01:45.629 --> 00:01:47.769
A decision becomes a snapshot. Click Approve. The exact version, policy, rationale, and expiry are preserved.
LAUNCH READY: Clearance applies only to the recorded scope.

12
00:01:47.769 --> 00:01:57.358
Policy changes have consequences. Sarah opens the policy library. Before publishing, the app shows how many approvals will need re-review.
CHANGE MANAGEMENT: Preview the impact before changing policy.

13
00:01:57.358 --> 00:02:09.151
Publish policy. Hold older approvals. The new inquiry-disclosure guidance immediately removes launch clearance from affected campaigns.
IMPORTANT DISTINCTION: Previously approved does not mean currently launch-ready.

14
00:02:09.151 --> 00:02:20.750
Stop distribution with a reason. The campaign displays its policy hold. Sarah also revokes the approval and records the next step.
CONTROL: Revocation removes clearance without deleting the decision.

15
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:28.345
The original decision survives. Open Versions: the previous approval snapshot is still available after revocation.
AUDITABILITY: History explains what was approved and why it later stopped.

16
00:02:28.345 --> 00:02:49.318
Now start with your own people. The sample identities stay available. Add Priya as a marketing owner and Morgan as a reviewer.
SELF-SERVE: These are browser-local identities, not authenticated accounts.

17
00:02:49.318 --> 00:02:55.148
Give the reviewer the right role. Enter Morgan’s name, choose Compliance reviewer, and save. Then switch to Priya.
SAME RULES: Custom people use the same workflow permissions as sample people.

18
00:02:55.148 --> 00:03:04.646
Create a campaign from scratch. Priya opens New submission and chooses Morgan as the independent reviewer.
BRING YOUR OWN WORK: Paste copy or import a local .txt / .md file.

19
00:03:04.646 --> 00:03:07.369
The brief adapts to its channel. Name the campaign and select Email. Affiliate destination requirements become optional.
CONTEXT MATTERS: Capture the actual distribution channel and scope.

20
00:03:07.369 --> 00:03:30.564
Your copy. Your team. Your audience. Priya enters the team, deadline, California audience, and campaign copy, then submits for review.
STRUCTURED INTAKE: A complete brief reduces avoidable reviewer follow-up.

21
00:03:30.564 --> 00:03:32.645
A real submission enters review. The new campaign is assigned to Morgan. Priya can see it, but cannot approve her own work.
EXTENSIBILITY: This is a working intake flow, not a fixed demo path.

22
00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:46.285
Automation will miss things. Morgan opens the campaign and adds a finding about disclosure prominence in the final email layout.
REVIEWER JUDGMENT: A text pre-check cannot assess visual placement.

23
00:03:46.285 --> 00:03:48.074
The same safeguards still apply. The manual finding is open and approval remains blocked until it is resolved with rationale.
CONSISTENCY: Custom campaigns follow the same evidence and decision gates.

24
00:03:48.074 --> 00:03:55.054
One visible chain of decisions. The audit joins the sample campaign and the new submission: revisions, evidence, approvals, and ownership.
PRODUCTION BOUNDARY: Browser-local data is editable. Production needs server-side roles and durable audit retention.
